The Pipe Chain
You can chain several steps and get something that reads top-to-bottom, roughly like “take mpg, then filter it, then select these columns”, instead of nesting function calls inside each other.

mpg |&gt;
  filter(class == "suv") |&gt;
  select(model, displ, hwy) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(hwy))







Note


This is R’s native pipe (base R ≥ 4.1). You may also see %&gt;% in older code or tutorials. They serve the same purpose.

Overriding the stat
When you already have the counts, switch to the identity stat (or use geom_col()):

diamonds |&gt;
  count(cut) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = cut, y = n)) +
  geom_bar(stat = "identity")

The extended template
ggplot(data = &lt;DATA&gt;) +
  &lt;GEOM_FUNCTION&gt;(
     mapping = aes(&lt;MAPPINGS&gt;),
     stat = &lt;STAT&gt;,
     position = &lt;POSITION&gt;
  ) +
  &lt;COORDINATE_FUNCTION&gt; +
  &lt;FACET_FUNCTION&gt;
There are seven slots. In practice you only fill in three: data, mappings, geom, because ggplot2 supplies sensible defaults for the rest.

The Antennae plot
Imagine describing human height to an alien who has never seen a person. A common way to compare two groups (popularized by spreadsheet software) is the dynamite plot: a bar at the group mean, with error-bar “antennae”.

heights |&gt;
  group_by(sex) |&gt;
  summarize(mean = mean(height), se = sd(height) / sqrt(n())) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = sex, y = mean)) +
  geom_col(fill = "grey70", width = 0.6) +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = mean, ymax = mean + 2 * se), width = 0.2) +
  labs(y = "Height (in)")

So how did we get here?
Everything on the last two slides came from one pipeline:
