Opening without loading
seattle_csv &lt;- open_dataset(
  sources = "data/seattle-library-checkouts.csv",
  col_types = schema(ISBN = string()),
  format = "csv"
)

open_dataset() scans a few thousand rows to learn the structure, then stops
The first 80,000 rows have blank ISBNs, so we specify that column’s type
Data is only read into memory as requested

seattle_csv |&gt;
  group_by(CheckoutYear) |&gt;
  summarise(Checkouts = sum(Checkouts)) |&gt;
  arrange(CheckoutYear) |&gt;
  collect()          # ~10 seconds on the CSV

dplyr with arrow
Same lazy pattern as dbplyr: build a query, collect() to run it.
query &lt;- seattle_pq |&gt;
  filter(CheckoutYear &gt;= 2018, MaterialType == "BOOK") |&gt;
  group_by(CheckoutYear, CheckoutMonth) |&gt;
  summarize(TotalCheckouts = sum(Checkouts)) |&gt;
  arrange(CheckoutYear, CheckoutMonth)

query |&gt; collect()
Like dbplyr, only some R expressions are supported; see ?acero for the list.

Arrow + duckdb
Hand an arrow dataset to duckdb without copying memory:
seattle_pq |&gt;
  to_duckdb() |&gt;
  filter(CheckoutYear &gt;= 2018, MaterialType == "BOOK") |&gt;
  group_by(CheckoutYear) |&gt;
  summarize(TotalCheckouts = sum(Checkouts)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(CheckoutYear)) |&gt;
  collect()
Arrow’s goal: seamless moves between computing environments.

Inconsistent types
What if a list-column mixes types?
df4 &lt;- tribble(
  ~x, ~y,
  "a", list(1),
  "b", list("a", TRUE, 5)
)

df4 |&gt; unnest_longer(y)
#&gt;   x     y
#&gt; 1 a     &lt;dbl [1]&gt;
#&gt; 2 b     &lt;chr [1]&gt;
#&gt; 3 b     &lt;lgl [1]&gt;
#&gt; 4 b     &lt;dbl [1]&gt;
unnest_longer() keeps the columns unchanged, so you get a list-column of length-1 vectors. Dealing with it needs tools from Chapter 26.

Case study 1: very wide data (gh_repos)
GitHub repos for six users, deeply nested.
repos &lt;- tibble(json = gh_repos)

repos |&gt;
  unnest_longer(json) |&gt;     # one row per repo (176 rows)
  unnest_wider(json)         # one column per field (68 columns!)
Pick the columns you want, then widen the nested owner:
repos |&gt;
  unnest_longer(json) |&gt;
  unnest_wider(json) |&gt;
  select(id, full_name, owner, description) |&gt;
  unnest_wider(owner, names_sep = "_")
owner also has an id, so without names_sep you get a duplicate-name error.

Case study 2: relational data (got_chars)
Nested data can stand in for several tables.
chars &lt;- tibble(json = got_chars)

characters &lt;- chars |&gt;
  unnest_wider(json) |&gt;
  select(id, name, gender, culture, born, died, alive)

titles &lt;- chars |&gt;
  unnest_wider(json) |&gt;
  select(id, titles) |&gt;
  unnest_longer(titles) |&gt;
  filter(titles != "") |&gt;
  rename(title = titles)
Repeat for aliases, allegiances, books, TV series, then join back to characters when needed.

Drilling into geometry
locations |&gt;
  select(city, formatted_address, geometry) |&gt;
  unnest_wider(geometry) |&gt;
  select(!location:viewport) |&gt;
  unnest_wider(bounds) |&gt;
  rename(ne = northeast, sw = southwest) |&gt;
  unnest_wider(c(ne, sw), names_sep = "_")
#&gt;   city    formatted_address  ne_lat ne_lng sw_lat sw_lng
Once you know the path, shortcut with hoist():
locations |&gt;
  select(city, formatted_address, geometry) |&gt;
  hoist(
    geometry,
    ne_lat = c("bounds", "northeast", "lat"),
    sw_lat = c("bounds", "southwest", "lat"),
    ne_lng = c("bounds", "northeast", "lng"),
    sw_lng = c("bounds", "southwest", "lng")
  )

HTML basics
Pages are a hierarchy of elements: start tag, optional attributes, contents, end tag.
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Page title&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1 id='first'&gt;A heading&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Some text &amp; &lt;b&gt;some bold text.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;img src='myimg.png' width='100' height='100'&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;

&lt;html&gt; → &lt;head&gt; (metadata) + &lt;body&gt; (content)
Block tags (&lt;h1&gt;, &lt;p&gt;, &lt;ol&gt;) structure the page; inline tags (&lt;b&gt;, &lt;a&gt;) format text
Key attributes: id, class, href, src
Escapes like &amp; are handled automatically by rvest

Getting the page into R
html &lt;- read_html("http://rvest.tidyverse.org/")
For examples, minimal_html() lets you write HTML inline:
html &lt;- minimal_html("
  &lt;h1&gt;This is a heading&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p id='first'&gt;This is a paragraph&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p class='important'&gt;This is an important paragraph&lt;/p&gt;
")

Case study: Star Wars films
Each film shares a structure:
&lt;section&gt;
  &lt;h2 data-id="1"&gt;The Phantom Menace&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Released: 1999-05-19&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Director: &lt;span class="director"&gt;George Lucas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;div class="crawl"&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
section &lt;- read_html(url) |&gt; html_elements("section")   # 7 films

tibble(
  title    = section |&gt; html_element("h2") |&gt; html_text2(),
  released = section |&gt; html_element("p") |&gt; html_text2() |&gt;
               str_remove("Released: ") |&gt; parse_date(),
  director = section |&gt; html_element(".director") |&gt; html_text2(),
  intro    = section |&gt; html_element(".crawl") |&gt; html_text2()
)
